# CHANGELOG.md is append-only: every PR adds an entry at the same spot under
# [Unreleased], so the first merge forces a rebase on every other open PR.
# The union driver keeps both sides' entries instead of conflicting.
# Deliberately scoped to this one file — union resolves by concatenation with no
# conflict, which is correct for a changelog and wrong for structured files.
#
# The driver is LOAD-BEARING, and that much is measured. This repo's workflow
# merges origin/main into each branch LOCALLY before the PR lands, and local git
# demonstrably honours the driver. Measured 2026-09-01 at main 28e20cd across
# all 73 open PRs, each replayed against its OWN base tip: 15 are clean with
# union and conflict without it, and in all 15 the ONLY conflicting file is
# CHANGELOG.md. Do not delete this line and do not migrate to changelog
# fragments.
#
# CAVEAT — github.com may still show "This branch has conflicts". Two separate
# reasons, and conflating them is what has repeatedly gone wrong here:
#
#   1. The mergeable flag is a STICKY-DIRTY CACHE. Once GitHub marks a PR
#      CONFLICTING, nothing re-evaluates it when the conflict later leaves the
#      base branch. Same 2026-09-01 sweep: 29 PRs read CONFLICTING and 15 of
#      them (52%) merge clean at their current base. Of the 14 that genuinely
#      conflict, ZERO conflict on CHANGELOG.md. So a red flag is an upper bound
#      that decays — never report a changelog-conflict backlog from it.
#
#   2. Whether GitHub's SERVER applies this driver is UNMEASURED here, in both
#      directions. This file used to assert "GitHub does not apply this rule"
#      as settled fact; that is withdrawn (WIC-1904), as was the symmetric
#      "38 of 38 proves it does" (WIC-1859). Both were base-coordinate
#      artifacts — they replayed PRs against today's main rather than against
#      the base GitHub actually used. The discriminating case (a PR GitHub
#      computes clean while it genuinely conflicts without union AT THAT SAME
#      BASE) is empty: all 15 union-dependent PRs above have a STALE
#      refs/pull/N/merge whose first parent is not the current base tip, so
#      they certify nothing either way. That is structural, not bad luck — the
#      local pre-merge sync below resolves the union seam before GitHub's
#      server merge ever sees it, so the case cannot arise in this workflow.
#      Argue from neither direction.
#
# Earlier figures in this file (WIC-1363, 2026-08-26: "of 30 open PRs, 25
# touched CHANGELOG.md and 17 read CONFLICTING") counted that sticky-dirty flag
# rather than conflicts, and are superseded by the sweep above.
#
# To decide whether a PR really is mergeable, use ground truth, never the flag
# alone: fetch refs/pull/N/merge and check it is a 2-parent commit with no
# conflict markers AND that its first parent is the current base tip, or run a
# real merge at the PR's current base in a throwaway worktree. If you use
# `git merge-tree`, --attr-source is a GLOBAL option and must come before the
# subcommand (`git --attr-source=<base> merge-tree ...`); placed after it, git
# exits 129, and omitted entirely it reads attributes from the working tree and
# measures nothing.
#
# So: do not go looking for a real conflict, and do not rebase a PR that others
# are stacked on. Merge main in and push — the driver resolves it on the way
# through, and the merge commit is what flips GitHub back to mergeable:
#
#     git fetch origin && git merge origin/main && git push
#
# If that leaves conflict markers in CHANGELOG.md, the driver did not fire (the
# file is only union-merged when .gitattributes is present in the checkout) and
# you are resolving by hand. Keep BOTH entries, newest date first; the sides are
# additive and neither edits the other's text.
CHANGELOG.md merge=union
